Meanwhile, if you're asking for ideas for a "flavorful kick" to add to a Thai curry, here are proven options (what they do and suggested amounts for a typical 4-serving curry):

- Thai red or green curry paste (1–2 tbsp) — the base heat and aromatics.  
- Nam prik pao (Thai roasted chili paste) (1 tbsp) — adds smoky sweet heat and depth.  
- Fish sauce (1–2 tsp) — umami and saltiness (use less if using soy).  
- Fresh lime juice or kaffir lime leaves (juice of 1 lime; 2–3 leaves) — bright acidity to lift the flavors.  
- Palm sugar or brown sugar (1 tsp) — balances heat and salt.  
- Fresh Thai basil or cilantro (handful) — fresh herb note at the end.  
- Sliced fresh chilies (bird’s eye) or chili flakes (to taste) — for extra heat.  
- Galangal or lemongrass (finely sliced, 1 tsp minced lemongrass or 1–2 thin slices galangal) — adds citrusy/peppery complexity.  
- A splash of coconut cream at the end — richer, silkier finish.

